special effects artists and prop  designers play a  huge role in creating movie magic they  find creative ways to ensure that props  look  as realistic as possible to find out how  these props are created  let's take a look at what 9 looked like  behind the scenes  prop glass is extremely fun to break and  is completely safe  this fake glass is made out of a corn  syrup mixture and  is sometimes referred to as candy glass  the glass can be molded into beer  bottles or flat panes  and the final product is brittle and  breaks easily and  safely however this fake glass would  still be uncomfortable to walk on  barefoot  like bruce willis had to do in die hard  so for scenes like that  glass shards made of rubber are used  instead  because there are strict rules for  having real babies on set  fake babies are sometimes used instead  in movies and tv shows  the babies are created by pouring  silicone into a mold  silicone is used because it has the same  slightly translucent quality as human  skin  after drying the babies are painted with  several different types of paint to make  their skin look like it's flush with  blood  then each hair on the baby's head is  added one at a time  it takes about three weeks to complete  just one fake baby  regular food could melt or go bad under  the hot lights of a movie set  over time so movie producers will often  have a prop designer create  fake foods for movies instead and they  get creative with materials  like using caulking for cake frosting or  corkboard for ground beef  to make most food items they first  create a mold based on the real food  and fill it with rubber or foam after  the prop has dried it's sanded down and  painted with details to look more  realistic  on certain occasions real food is simply  covered in resin to help preserve it  fake vomit comes in all different shapes  sizes and textures  it can be realistic or exaggerated  depending on the genre  creating fake vomit starts by mixing  together different types of foods  for a more traditional vomit they  combine top ramen  and tomato paste  actors will put some of this mixture  into their mouths before the beginning  of the shot  before spitting it out on cue but to  create a more dramatic vomit scene  the fake vomit is shot out of a tube  the thicker part of this food mixture is  loaded into one end  the watery part is pumped into the tube  as well  pushing out the solids and creating a  vomit explosion  to create more exaggerated vomiting  small holes can be added to the end of  the tubing to produce a larger spray  sometimes these tubes are removed in  post-production  while other times it's all about picking  the right camera angle  the blood sugar levels of this fake  blood is through the roof  since fake blood is almost entirely  composed of corn syrup  the only other ingredients are an o  pacifier to make the blood cloudier  and food coloring a little bit of blue  food coloring is added to the red to  darken it and add complexity to the  mixture  the fake blood can then be shot out of a  cannon if you need to simulate a  headshot  or sprayed if the fake wound is the  result of a slash  rubber blood can also be used when  filming needs to be stain free  the easiest way to create a fake burn  wound is with  a simple temporary tattoo they stay on  all day  and they're easy to apply but they're  not the most realistic  so for more complicated burn wounds  makeup artists will typically use  glutzen bald cap plastic this plastic  can either be painted  or burned to give it a singed appearance  the bald cap plastic is then applied to  the skin with an adhesive  and the edges are painted to blend in  for larger burn areas  sheets of silicone can be used the  silicone is molded  painted and then applied directly onto  the actor  fake body parts are a necessary prop for  many tv shows and movies  especially those that involve gore  these fake bodies are typically created  by filling molds with polyphone  sometimes an actor will have to go  through a life casting  which creates a perfect mold of their  facial features  the polyfoam body parts are then layered  with paint  the expert painters can replicate all  manner of injuries  from shotgun wounds to frostbite  whether you need a snow storm a huge  gust of wind or a rain shower  practical effects artists have you  covered because you can't always count  on the weather  movies and tv shows often need to come  up with other ways to create  fake weather for a scene giant fans are  used for wind  and piping rigs can produce realistic  raindrops  to emulate light snow small pieces of  paper mache or plastic are blown through  this machine  sometimes a special soap-based foam is  used instead  which doesn't stain or make the ground  slippery  the hairs on fake beards and wigs are  often threaded by hand  one at a time this means that making a  fake beard can sometimes take  up to six days to create these pieces  hairs are either punched into a  prosthesis one at a time  or are individually woven into a lace  foundation  however if time is of the essence fake  hair can also be applied directly to an  actor's face with glue  not only do hair artists need to  consider how they're going to apply hair  to an after  but also what kind of hair they're going  to use rough yak hair  is good for beards and finer angora hair  is used for eyebrows  what are some of your favorite movie  props and practical effects  let us know in the comments
 

